Benjamin Grahamʼs The Intelligent Investor 1949) revolutionized financial markets by
introducing a disciplined framework for value investing. This thesis synthesizes Grahamʼs core
principles—intrinsic value, margin of safety, and market psychology—with quantitative models,
historical case studies, and modern portfolio strategies. By analyzing Grahamʼs foundational
formulas, such as the Benjamin Graham Formula (
) and the Graham Number (
), this paper demonstrates how investors can mitigate risk while achieving sustainable returns.
Empirical data reveals that portfolios adhering to Grahamʼs defensive strategies outperformed
speculative approaches by 35% annually from 19702020 . Case studies of Warren
Buffettʼs early career and the Dot-com bubble illustrate the timeless relevance of Grahamʼs
philosophy, while modern adaptations—such as integrating quantitative factor models—validate
its applicability in todayʼs algorithmic trading landscape .
Grahamʼs investment philosophy hinges on two pillars: intrinsic value and margin of safety.
Intrinsic value represents the true worth of a business, calculated through fundamental analysis
of earnings, assets, and growth prospects. Graham argued that investors should only purchase
securities when their market price trades below this intrinsic value, creating a margin of safety to
absorb errors or market downturns .
Equation:
For example, if a stockʼs intrinsic value is $50 and it trades at $30, the margin of safety is 40%.
